Article 9 For the public issuance of securities, the conditions prescribed by laws and administrative regulations must be met, and registration must be reported to the securities regulatory authority under the State Council or the department authorized by the State Council according to law. No entity or individual may publicly issue securities without lawful registration. The specific scope and implementation steps of the securities issuance registration system shall be prescribed by the State Council.
Any of the following circumstances shall constitute a public issuance:
(1) Issuing securities to unspecified persons;
(2) Issuing securities to specified persons with an aggregate of more than 200 persons, provided that the number of employees under an employee stock ownership plan implemented according to law shall not be counted;
(3) Other issuance acts prescribed by laws or administrative regulations.
For non-public issuance of securities, no advertisement, public solicitation, or disguised public methods may be used.

Article 11 To establish a company limited by shares and publicly issue stocks, it shall meet the conditions prescribed by the Company Law of the People's Republic of China and other conditions prescribed by the securities regulatory authority under the State Council approved by the State Council, and submit the share offering application and the following documents to the securities regulatory authority under the State Council:
(1) The company's articles of association;
(2) The promoter agreement;
(3) The names or titles of the promoters, the number of shares subscribed by the promoters, the types of capital contributions, and the capital verification certificate;
(4) The prospectus;
(5) The name and address of the bank entrusted to collect the share proceeds;
(6) The name of the underwriting institution and relevant agreements.
If a sponsor is engaged in accordance with this Law, the issuance sponsorship letter issued by the sponsor shall also be submitted.
If the establishment of the company requires approval by laws or administrative regulations, the corresponding approval documents shall also be submitted.

Article 12 A company making an initial public offering of new shares shall meet the following conditions:
(1) Having a sound and well-functioning organizational structure;
(2) Having sustainable business capacity;
(3) Having unqualified audit opinions on the financial accounting reports for the last three years;
(4) The issuer and its controlling shareholders and actual controllers have no criminal records of embezzlement, bribery, misappropriation of property, or crimes disrupting the order of the socialist market economy in the last three years;
(5) Other conditions prescribed by the securities regulatory authority under the State Council approved by the State Council.
A listed company issuing new shares shall meet the conditions prescribed by the securities regulatory authority under the State Council approved by the State Council, and the specific administrative measures shall be prescribed by the securities regulatory authority under the State Council.
For a public issuance of depositary receipts, it shall meet the conditions for an initial public offering of new shares and other conditions prescribed by the securities regulatory authority under the State Council.

Article 13 A company publicly issuing new shares shall submit a share offering application and the following documents:
(1) The company's business license;
(2) The company's articles of association;
(3) The resolution of the shareholders' meeting;
(4) The prospectus or other public issuance offering documents;
(5) The financial accounting report;
(6) The name and address of the bank entrusted to collect the share proceeds.
If a sponsor is engaged in accordance with this Law, the issuance sponsorship letter issued by the sponsor shall also be submitted. If underwriting is adopted in accordance with this Law, the name of the underwriting institution and relevant agreements shall also be submitted.

Article 15 A public issuance of corporate bonds shall meet the following conditions:
(1) Having a sound and well-functioning organizational structure;
(2) Having average distributable profits over the last three years sufficient to pay one year's interest on the corporate bonds;
(3) Other conditions prescribed by the State Council.
The funds raised from the public issuance of corporate bonds must be used in accordance with the purposes listed in the corporate bond offering method; any change in the use of funds must be resolved by the bondholders' meeting. The funds raised from the public issuance of corporate bonds shall not be used to cover losses or for non-productive expenditures.
For a listed company issuing corporate bonds convertible into stocks, in addition to meeting the conditions set forth in the first paragraph, it shall also comply with the provisions of Article 12, paragraph 2 of this Law, unless the corporate bond offering method contemplates conversion through the listed company's repurchase of its own shares.

Article 16 An application for public issuance of corporate bonds shall be submitted to the department authorized by the State Council or the securities regulatory authority under the State Council with the following documents:
(1) The company's business license;
(2) The company's articles of association;
(3) The corporate bond offering method;
(4) Other documents prescribed by the department authorized by the State Council or the securities regulatory authority under the State Council.
If a sponsor is engaged in accordance with this Law, the issuance sponsorship letter issued by the sponsor shall also be submitted.

Article 17 Under any of the following circumstances, no further public issuance of corporate bonds may be made:
(1) There is a fact of default or delay in payment of principal and interest on previously publicly issued corporate bonds or other debts, and such fact is still continuing;
(2) In violation of the provisions of this Law, the use of funds raised from the public issuance of corporate bonds has been changed.

Article 28 When a securities company underwrites securities, it shall enter into an agency underwriting or firm commitment underwriting agreement with the issuer, specifying the following matters:
(1) names, domiciles, and names of legal representatives of the parties;
(2) types, quantities, amounts, and offering prices of the securities to be underwritten on an agency or firm commitment basis;
(3) term and start and end dates of the agency underwriting or firm commitment underwriting;
(4) payment methods and dates for agency underwriting or firm commitment underwriting;
(5) fees and settlement methods for agency underwriting or firm commitment underwriting;
(6) liability for breach of contract;
(7) other matters prescribed by the securities regulatory authority of the State Council.

Article 29 When underwriting securities, a securities company shall verify the truthfulness, accuracy, and completeness of the public offering documents. If it discovers any false records, misleading statements, or material omissions, it shall not carry out sales activities; if sales have already been made, it must immediately cease sales activities and take corrective measures.
When underwriting securities, a securities company shall not engage in the following acts:
(1) conducting false advertising or other promotional activities that mislead investors;
(2) soliciting underwriting business by unfair competition means;
(3) other acts that violate the provisions on securities underwriting business.
If a securities company commits any of the acts listed in the preceding paragraph and causes losses to other securities underwriting institutions or investors, it shall bear compensation liability according to law.

Article 64 An announcement made in accordance with the preceding article shall include the following:
(1) the name and domicile of the person holding the shares;
(2) the name and quantity of the stocks held;
(3) the date on which the shareholding reaches the statutory proportion or the change in shareholding reaches the statutory proportion, and the source of funds for the increase in shareholding;
(4) the time and manner of changes in the shares with voting rights held in the listed company.

Article 66 Where a takeover offer is made in accordance with the preceding article, the acquirer must announce the listed company acquisition report, which shall specify the following:
(1) the name and domicile of the acquirer;
(2) the decision of the acquirer regarding the acquisition;
(3) the name of the acquired listed company;
(4) the purpose of the acquisition;
(5) the detailed names of the shares to be acquired and the proposed number of shares to be acquired;
(6) the acquisition period and acquisition price;
(7) the amount of funds required for the acquisition and the guarantee of funds;
(8) the proportion of the shares held in the acquired company to the total issued shares of that company at the time of announcing the listed company acquisition report.

Article 68 During the acceptance period specified in the takeover offer, the offeror shall not revoke its takeover offer. Where the offeror needs to modify the takeover offer, it shall make a timely announcement specifying the specific modifications, and shall not have any of the following circumstances:
(1) reducing the acquisition price;
(2) reducing the number of shares scheduled to be purchased;
(3) shortening the acquisition period;
(4) other circumstances prescribed by the securities regulatory authority under the State Council.

Article 79 A listed company, a company whose corporate bonds are listed for trading, and a company whose shares are traded on other national securities trading venues approved by the State Council shall prepare periodic reports in accordance with the content and format prescribed by the securities regulatory authority under the State Council and the securities trading venues, and shall submit and announce them according to the following provisions:
(1) within four months after the end of each fiscal year, submit and announce the annual report, wherein the annual financial accounting report shall be audited by an accounting firm that meets the requirements of this Law;
(2) within two months after the end of the first half of each fiscal year, submit and announce the interim report.

Article 80 Where a major event occurs that may have a significant impact on the trading price of shares of a listed company or of a company whose shares are traded on other national securities trading venues approved by the State Council, and the investors have not yet learned of it, the company shall immediately submit a temporary report on the relevant circumstances of the major event to the securities regulatory authority under the State Council and the securities trading venue, and make an announcement, explaining the cause of the event, its current status, and the possible legal consequences.
The term "major event" in the preceding paragraph includes:
(1) major changes in the company's business policy and business scope;
(2) major investment behaviors of the company, where the company purchases or sells major assets exceeding thirty percent of the company's total assets within one year, or where the mortgage, pledge, sale, or scrapping of the company's main assets used for business operations exceeds thirty percent of such assets at one time;
(3) the company enters into important contracts, provides major guarantees, or engages in related-party transactions which may have a significant impact on the company's assets, liabilities, equity, and operating results;
(4) the company incurs major debts or defaults on major debts that have fallen due;
(5) the company suffers major losses or major damages;
(6) major changes in the external conditions for the company's production and operation;
(7) changes in the directors, more than one-third of the supervisors, or the manager of the company, or the chairman of the board or the manager is unable to perform duties;
(8) significant changes in the shareholding of shareholders holding five percent or more of the company's shares or in the control of the company by the actual controller, or significant changes in the situation where the actual controller and other enterprises controlled by it engage in the same or similar business as the company;
(9) plans for distribution of dividends or capital increase, important changes in the company's equity structure, decisions to reduce capital, merge, divide, dissolve, or apply for bankruptcy, or entering into bankruptcy proceedings according to law, or being ordered to close down;
(10) major litigation or arbitration involving the company, or resolutions of the shareholders' general meeting or the board of directors being revoked or declared invalid according to law;
(11) the company is placed on file for investigation on suspicion of crime according to law, or the controlling shareholder, actual controller, directors, supervisors, or senior officers of the company are subjected to compulsory measures according to law on suspicion of crime;
(12) other matters prescribed by the securities regulatory authority under the State Council.
Where the controlling shareholder or the actual controller of the company has a significant influence on the occurrence or development of a major event, it shall promptly inform the company in writing of the relevant information it has learned, and cooperate with the company in fulfilling its information disclosure obligations.

Article 81 Where a major event occurs that may have a significant impact on the trading price of listed corporate bonds, and the investors have not yet learned of it, the company shall immediately submit a temporary report on the relevant circumstances of the major event to the securities regulatory authority under the State Council and the securities trading venue, and make an announcement, explaining the cause of the event, its current status, and the possible legal consequences.
The term "major event" in the preceding paragraph includes:
(1) major changes in the company's equity structure or production and operation status;
(2) changes in the credit rating of the company's bonds;
(3) mortgage, pledge, sale, transfer, or scrapping of major assets of the company;
(4) the company's failure to pay debts that have fallen due;
(5) the company's new borrowings or external guarantees exceeding twenty percent of the net assets at the end of the preceding year;
(6) the company's waiver of claims or property exceeding ten percent of the net assets at the end of the preceding year;
(7) the company suffers major losses exceeding ten percent of the net assets at the end of the preceding year;
(8) the company distributes dividends, makes decisions to reduce capital, merge, divide, dissolve, or apply for bankruptcy, or enters into bankruptcy proceedings according to law, or is ordered to close down;
(9) major litigation or arbitration involving the company;
(10) the company is placed on file for investigation on suspicion of crime according to law, or the controlling shareholder, actual controller, directors, supervisors, or senior officers of the company are subjected to compulsory measures according to law on suspicion of crime;
(11) other matters prescribed by the securities regulatory authority under the State Council.

Article 103 A person who falls under the circumstances provided in Article 146 of the Company Law of the People's Republic of China or under any of the following circumstances shall not serve as a responsible person of a stock exchange:

(1) A person who has been removed from the position of responsible person of a securities trading venue or securities registration and settlement institution, or director, supervisor, or senior officer of a securities company due to illegal or disciplinary acts, where less than five years have elapsed since the date of removal;

(2) A lawyer, certified public accountant, or other professional of a securities service institution whose practice certificate has been revoked or whose qualification has been canceled due to illegal or disciplinary acts, where less than five years have elapsed since the date of revocation or cancellation.

Article 118 The establishment of a securities company shall meet the following conditions and be subject to the approval of the securities regulatory authority under the State Council:
(1) Having a company's articles of association that comply with the provisions of laws and administrative regulations;
(2) The principal shareholders and the actual controller of the company have good financial conditions and integrity records, and have no record of major violations of laws or regulations in the recent three years;
(3) Having registered capital that meets the provisions of this Law;
(4) The directors, supervisors, senior officers, and employees meet the conditions as provided for in this Law;
(5) Having a sound risk management and internal control system;
(6) Having qualified business premises, business facilities, and information technology systems;
(7) Other conditions as prescribed by laws, administrative regulations, and the securities regulatory authority under the State Council as approved by the State Council.
Without the approval of the securities regulatory authority under the State Council, no entity or individual may conduct securities business activities in the name of a securities company.

Article 120 Upon verification and approval by the securities regulatory authority under the State Council and obtaining a license to engage in securities business, a securities company may engage in the following part or all of the securities businesses:
(1) Securities brokerage;
(2) Securities investment consulting;
(3) Financial advisory relating to securities trading and securities investment activities;
(4) Securities underwriting and sponsorship;
(5) Securities margin trading;
(6) Securities market making;
(7) Securities proprietary trading;
(8) Other securities businesses.
The securities regulatory authority under the State Council shall, within three months from the date of accepting an application for the matters specified in the preceding paragraph, conduct a review in accordance with the statutory conditions and procedures, make a decision to verify and approve or disapprove, and notify the applicant; if the application is disapproved, the reasons shall be stated.
If a securities company engages in securities asset management business, it shall comply with the provisions of the Securities Investment Fund Law of the People's Republic of China and other laws and administrative regulations.
Except for securities companies, no entity or individual may engage in securities underwriting, securities sponsorship, securities brokerage, or securities margin trading.
A securities company engaging in securities margin trading shall take measures to strictly prevent and control risks, and shall not lend funds or securities to clients in violation of regulations.

Article 140 Where the governance structure, compliance management or risk control indicators of a securities company do not conform to the provisions, the securities regulatory authority of the State Council shall order it to make corrections within a time limit; if it fails to make corrections within the time limit, or its conduct seriously endangers the sound operation of the securities company or damages the lawful rights and interests of clients, the securities regulatory authority of the State Council may, depending on the circumstances, take the following measures with respect to it:
(1) Restrict its business activities, order the suspension of part of its business, and cease approving new businesses;
(2) Restrict the distribution of dividends and restrict the payment of remuneration and provision of benefits to directors, supervisors and senior officers;
(3) Restrict the transfer of property or the creation of other rights over property;
(4) Order the replacement of directors, supervisors or senior officers or restrict their rights;
(5) Revoke relevant business permits;
(6) Identify the responsible directors, supervisors and senior officers as unsuitable persons; and
(7) Order the responsible shareholders to transfer their equity and restrict the responsible shareholders from exercising their shareholders' rights.
After the securities company has made rectifications, it shall submit a report to the securities regulatory authority of the State Council. Where, upon inspection, the securities regulatory authority of the State Council finds that the governance structure, compliance management and risk control indicators conform to the provisions, it shall, within three days from the completion of the inspection, lift the relevant restrictive measures taken against it under the preceding paragraph.

Article 147 A securities registration and settlement institution shall perform the following functions:
(1) Establishment of securities accounts and settlement accounts;
(2) Depository and transfer of securities;
(3) Registration of the register of securities holders;
(4) Clearing and settlement of securities transactions;
(5) Distribution of securities rights and interests upon entrustment by the issuer;
(6) Handling of inquiries and information services relating to the above businesses; and
(7) Other businesses approved by the securities regulatory authority of the State Council.

Article 152 A securities registration and settlement institution shall take the following measures to ensure the normal operation of its business:
(1) having necessary service equipment and complete data security protection measures;
(2) establishing complete management systems for business, finance, and security prevention;
(3) establishing a complete risk management system.

Article 161 A securities investment consulting institution and its practitioners shall not engage in the following acts when providing securities services:
(1) acting as an agent for the client in securities investment;
(2) agreeing with the client to share securities investment returns or bear securities investment losses;
(3) buying or selling securities for which this securities investment consulting institution provides services;
(4) other acts prohibited by laws and administrative regulations.
Where any act listed in the preceding paragraph causes losses to investors, the institution or practitioner shall bear compensation liability according to law.

Article 166 The Securities Association performs the following duties:
(1) educating and organizing members and their practitioners to comply with securities laws and administrative regulations, organizing the construction of good faith in the securities industry, and urging the securities industry to fulfill social responsibilities;
(2) safeguarding the lawful rights and interests of members according to law, and reflecting members' suggestions and requirements to the securities regulatory authority;
(3) urging members to carry out investor education and protection activities, and safeguarding the lawful rights and interests of investors;
(4) formulating and implementing self-regulatory rules for the securities industry, supervising and inspecting the conduct of members and their practitioners, and imposing disciplinary sanctions or implementing other self-regulatory management measures in accordance with the provisions for violations of laws, administrative regulations, self-regulatory rules, or the articles of association;
(5) formulating business standards for the securities industry and organizing professional training for practitioners;
(6) organizing members to conduct research on the development, operation, and related content of the securities industry, collecting, compiling, and publishing securities-related information, providing member services, organizing industry exchanges, and guiding industry innovation and development;
(7) mediating securities business disputes between members or between members and clients;
(8) other duties as stipulated in the articles of association of the Securities Association.

Article 169 When exercising supervision and administration over the securities market, the securities regulatory authority of the State Council shall perform the following duties:
(1) to formulate, according to law, rules and regulations concerning the supervision and administration of the securities market, and to conduct examination, approval, registration and record-filing according to law;
(2) to exercise supervision and administration, according to law, over acts such as the issuance, listing, trading, registration, depository and clearing of securities;
(3) to exercise supervision and administration, according to law, over the securities business activities of securities issuers, securities companies, securities service institutions, securities trading venues and securities registration and clearing institutions;
(4) to formulate, according to law, codes of conduct for persons engaged in securities business, and supervise the implementation thereof;
(5) to supervise and inspect, according to law, the information disclosure in securities issuance, listing and trading;
(6) to guide and supervise, according to law, the self-regulatory activities of the Securities Industry Association;
(7) to monitor, prevent and handle, according to law, securities market risks;
(8) to carry out, according to law, investor education;
(9) to investigate and deal with, according to law, securities violations;
(10) other duties as prescribed by laws and administrative regulations.

Article 170 When performing its duties according to law, the securities regulatory authority of the State Council shall have the authority to take the following measures:
(1) to conduct on-site inspection of securities issuers, securities companies, securities service institutions, securities trading venues and securities registration and clearing institutions;
(2) to enter the places where suspected illegal acts are committed to investigate and collect evidence;
(3) to inquire of the parties, and the entities and individuals relevant to the event under investigation, and require them to give explanations on matters relevant to the event under investigation; or require them to submit documents and materials relevant to the event under investigation in a specified manner;
(4) to consult and copy documents and materials such as property right registration and communication records relevant to the event under investigation;
(5) to consult and copy the securities trading records, registration and transfer records, financial accounting materials and other relevant documents and materials of the parties, and the entities and individuals relevant to the event under investigation; in respect of documents and materials that may be transferred, concealed or destroyed, may seal them up or seize them;
(6) to inquire about the information of fund accounts, securities accounts, bank accounts and other accounts with payment, custody, settlement and other functions of the parties, and the entities and individuals relevant to the event under investigation, and may copy the relevant documents and materials; where there is evidence proving that illegal funds, securities or other involved property have been or may be transferred or concealed, or that important evidence has been concealed, forged or destroyed, may, upon approval by the principal person in charge of the securities regulatory authority of the State Council or other person in charge authorized thereby, freeze or seal them up for a term of six months; where extension is necessary due to special reasons, the term of each extension shall not exceed three months, and the maximum term of freezing or sealing up shall not exceed two years;
(7) when investigating major securities violations such as market manipulation and insider trading, may, upon approval by the principal person in charge of the securities regulatory authority of the State Council or other person in charge authorized thereby, restrict the securities trading of the parties under investigation, provided that the term of restriction shall not exceed three months; where the case is complicated, the term may be extended by three months;
(8) to notify the exit-entry administration authority to prevent, according to law, the suspected violators, the persons directly in charge of the suspected violating entities and other persons directly liable from leaving the country.
For the purpose of preventing securities market risks and maintaining market order, the securities regulatory authority of the State Council may take measures such as ordering rectification, supervisory conversations and issuing warning letters.

Article 216 Where the securities regulatory authority of the State Council or the department authorized by the State Council has any of the following circumstances, the directly responsible persons in charge and other directly liable persons shall be given sanctions according to law:
(1) approving, registering, or permitting an application that does not comply with the provisions of this Law, such as an application for issuing securities or establishing a securities company;
(2) taking measures such as on-site inspection, investigation and evidence collection, inquiry, freezing, or sealing up in violation of the provisions of this Law;
(3) taking supervision and administration measures against relevant institutions and persons in violation of the provisions of this Law;
(4) imposing administrative penalties on relevant institutions and persons in violation of the provisions of this Law;
(5) other acts of failing to perform their duties according to law.
